    "updated_at": "2018-05-23T11:26:33Z",
    "body": "For me it started with not being able to use Atom to edit the file. Although it's fair to say it's Atom's fault for chocking at a file that is not THAT big. :) But Github also gives up showing the source file.\r\n\r\nThe hellish task for me is: I found something on multipage that I want to edit. Locating it on **source** involves searching for a phrase-long string that has no ```<data>``` or any type of annotation, which usually means a generic sentence like *\"the user agent should allow\"* which returns 20 results and I have to read a block of text to try to map what I saw on the web page with the source until I find the region I want to edit.\r\n\r\nI don't know how everybody does it, but that's always the worst part for me. Sometimes it's just *\"Oh, I have to change that on the 'OffscreenCanvas' section\"*. Good luck searching for that. :)\r\n\r\nDo you mean like search and replace type of tasks? That doesn't sound too complicated with multiple files.\r\n\r\nBut even those examples you gave. From a pure repo perspective, I'd argue that having them as separate files on the the same repo would probably be better than having them on separate repos as they are today. On the other hand, they probably do benefit from having separate issue/pr tracking.",
    "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
